"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_biology_en"][2]={"university_biology_en:M2-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M2-L1 – Functions, derivatives and growth models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics, Statistics and Data Literacy<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> School mathematics: powers,\nexponential functions and linear equations; <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish linear, exponential\nand logistic growth models, interpret derivatives biologically and check\nparameter units.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A population may initially grow rapidly and later approach a stable\nlevel. Absolute increase, relative growth rate and long-term limitation\ndescribe different features of the same trajectory. The choice of\nfunction depends on the assumed process, not merely on visual agreement\nwith measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<p>Linear growth follows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)=N_0+at<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\nequal time intervals add equal numbers of individuals. Its derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dN/dt=a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant. Exponential growth instead satisfies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dN/dt=rN<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith solution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)=N_0e^{rt}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe relative rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(dN/dt)/N=r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant. If\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ndoubling time is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(2)/r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe modelled quantity decreases. With time in days, the unit of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>r<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis day⁻¹.<\/p>\n<p>The logistic differential equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>r<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dN/dt=rN(1-N/K)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes a simple density-dependent limitation. Carrying capacity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the same unit as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>N<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;N&lt;K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases but approaches\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=K/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nabsolute growth rate is maximal at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">rK/4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe derivative vanishes. The model assumes constant capacity and\nimmediate feedback.<\/p>\n<p>The derivative gives local slope, whereas the difference quotient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta N/\\Delta t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndescribes the average rate over an interval. In measured data the latter\napproximates the former; measurement noise can strongly affect the\nestimate.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_006_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential and logistic models with N₀=10, r=0.5 per day, K=100.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponential and logistic models with\nN₀=10, r=0.5 per day, K=100.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Exponential and logistic models with N₀=10, r=0.5 per\nday, K=100.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>A fictional model uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N_0=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nday⁻¹ and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter four days, exponential growth gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>73.89<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10e^2\\approx73.89<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nLogistic growth gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N(t)=K/[1+(K/N_0-1)e^{-rt}]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>100<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>9<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>45.09<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">100/(1+9e^{-2})\\approx45.09<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe curves in the figure use these exact parameters. At\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nlogistic growth gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>12.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\cdot50\\cdot(1-0.5)=12.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindividuals per day as the instantaneous rate. Fractional model values\nare continuous approximations, not counted individuals.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate exponential doubling time for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nday⁻¹.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Find instantaneous logistic growth rate at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N=80<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which model assumption is violated if carrying capacity fluctuates\nseasonally?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">ln<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.39<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ln(2)/0.5\\approx1.39<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndays.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>100<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\cdot80\\cdot(1-80/100)=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindividuals per day.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Capacity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>K<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not constant; a time-dependent model or another resource model is\nrequired.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The deterministic model includes neither random births and deaths nor\nage structure, migration or delayed competition. Fitting short time\nseries is insufficient to distinguish exponential and logistic growth\nreliably over the long term.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Growth functions connect population size, rate and resource\nlimitation. M2-L2 introduces randomness into biological models.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/45-3-environmental-limits-to-population-growth\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 45.3 Environmental Limits to Population Growth<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nExponential and logistic growth · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M2-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M2-L2 – Probability and distributions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics, Statistics and Data Literacy<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> Basic arithmetic, powers and\nfractions; <a href=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish events, conditional\nprobability and random variables; calculate binomial counts and assess\nmodel assumptions.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Not every seed in a batch germinates. Probability describes\nuncertainty about the result of an experiment under particular\nconditions. It does not specify which individual seed will\ngerminate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<p>For events\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>A<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid B)=P(A\\cap B)/P(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(B)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder independence,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∩<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nMutually exclusive events of positive probability are not independent.\nComplementary probability is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>A<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A^c)=1-P(A)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<p>A random variable assigns numbers to outcomes. A Bernoulli variable\ntakes 1 with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand otherwise 0. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent trials with identical\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis binomial:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=k)=\\binom{n}{k}p^k(1-p)^{n-k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe expectation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E(X)=np<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a long-run average; variance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Var}(X)=np(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmeasures dispersion. Standard deviation is its square root. A continuous\ndistribution, such as the normal distribution, instead assigns\nprobabilities to intervals; a single exact value has zero\nprobability.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_007_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Model probabilities for four independent seeds with p=0.75.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Model probabilities for four independent\nseeds with p=0.75.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Model probabilities for four independent seeds with\np=0.75.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Four independent fictional seeds germinate with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\neach. The probability of exactly three seedlings is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.421875<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X=3)=4(0.75)^3(0.25)=0.421875<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe probability of at least one seedling is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.99609375<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-(0.25)^4=0.99609375<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nExpectation is 3 and variance 0.75. The diagram shows all five possible\ncounts. It uses model probabilities rather than observations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the probability that all four seeds germinate.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the standard deviation of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why might shared culture in a single dish violate the binomial\nassumption?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.31640625<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(0.75)^4=0.31640625<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.866<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{0.75}\\approx0.866<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nseedlings.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Shared environmental conditions may correlate outcomes; independent\ntrials and constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nare then not guaranteed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Probabilities apply within the specified model. Without empirical\nestimation or a justified assumption,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis not a claim about a real seed batch. Variable individual\nprobabilities or dependent outcomes require other models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Random variables and distributions underpin description and\nsubsequent inference from samples.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/4-3-binomial-distribution\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Statistics 2e, 4.3 Binomial Distribution<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Binomial\nassumptions, mean and variance · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M2-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M2-L3 – Descriptive statistics<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics, Statistics and Data Literacy<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L2<\/a>; sums and square\nroots.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Describe location, dispersion\nand distribution shape; calculate mean, median and sample standard\ndeviation from biological measurements.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>Five plants of the same species have different shoot lengths. A\nsingle average length can obscure these differences. A descriptive\nanalysis should therefore show measurements, units, sample size and\nappropriate summaries together.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<p>For quantitative values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1,\\ldots,x_n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x=\\sum_i x_i/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe median divides ordered data into two halves; for an even number,\nconventionally take the mean of the two middle values. The median is\nless sensitive to isolated extreme observations. Range is maximum minus\nminimum. The interquartile range spans the first and third quartiles;\nfor small samples, calculation conventions differ.<\/p>\n<p>Sample variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2/(n-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder independent identical distributions and finite variance, it is an\nunbiased estimator of population variance. Sample standard deviation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>s<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas the original measurement unit. It is not the standard error of the\nmean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhich estimates the mean’s sampling uncertainty under relevant\nassumptions. Dot plots show individual values, histograms show class\nfrequencies and box plots show quartiles. For categorical data,\nfrequencies and proportions are more suitable than means of arbitrary\ncategory codes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_008_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Dot plot of fictional shoot lengths 2, 3, 3, 4 and 8 cm.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Dot plot of fictional shoot lengths 2, 3,\n3, 4 and 8 cm.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Dot plot of fictional shoot lengths 2, 3, 3, 4 and 8\ncm.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional shoot lengths in cm: 2, 3, 3, 4, 8. Sum 20, mean 4 cm and\nmedian 3 cm. Squared deviations from the mean are 4, 1, 1, 0, 16; hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>s<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>22<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s^2=22/4=5.5\\,\\mathrm{cm^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.35<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s\\approx2.35<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncm. Range is 6 cm. The cluster at 3 and high value 8 are directly\nvisible in the dot plot. The value 8 should not be excluded as an error\nmerely because it is unusual.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate mean and median without the value 8 as a sensitivity\nanalysis only.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate the estimated standard error of the original mean under\nindependence.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Name one advantage of a dot plot with only five measurements.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>12<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">12/4=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncm; median 3 cm. This calculation does not justify exclusion.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mn>5.5<\/mn><\/msqrt><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>5<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{5.5}/\\sqrt5\\approx1.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncm.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Every measurement and repeated value is visible; small sample size\nis not concealed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Descriptive statistics alone cannot establish causality or\nrepresentativeness. Interpretation depends on measurement quality,\nsampling design and selection process.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Descriptive measures characterize observed data. Confidence intervals\nand tests additionally incorporate sampling uncertainty.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/2-5-measures-of-the-center-of-the-data\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Statistics 2e, 2.5 Measures of the Center of the Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Mean and median · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/2-7-measures-of-the-spread-of-the-data\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Statistics 2e, 2.7 Measures of the Spread of the Data<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a>\n· Sample standard deviation and variance · Last accessed:\n2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M2-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M2-L4 – Hypothesis tests and confidence intervals<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics, Statistics and Data Literacy<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a>: mean, dispersion and\nstandard error.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate a confidence interval\nfor a mean, interpret a two-sided t test and distinguish statistical\nfrom biological relevance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A difference between measured and expected leaf length may arise from\nsampling variation or a real difference in the underlying mean.\nInference connects measurements to an explicitly chosen model of this\nuncertainty.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<p>For independent identically normally distributed observations with\nunknown population variance, the two-sided\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1-\\alpha)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nconfidence interval for the mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>±<\/mo><msub><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar x\\pm t_{n-1,1-\\alpha/2}s/\\sqrt n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe critical value comes from Student’s t distribution with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndegrees of freedom. A 95% confidence procedure produces intervals\ncontaining the true mean in 95% of repeated samples under the model\nassumptions. The interval already calculated does not assign a\nsubsequent probability of 95% to the fixed parameter.<\/p>\n<p>A two-sided test of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>:<\/mo><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0:\\mu=\\mu_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mi>n<\/mi><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=(\\bar x-\\mu_0)/(s/\\sqrt n)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe p value is the probability, under\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the model assumptions, of a test statistic at least as extreme as\nobserved. It is not the probability that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis true. With prespecified\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>α<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nreject\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>H<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\leq\\alpha<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA type I error is rejection of a true null hypothesis; a type II error\nis failure to reject a false null hypothesis. Power is one minus the\nprobability of a type II error for a specified alternative.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_009_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"95% confidence interval from 9.869 to 14.131 mm; reference value 10 mm lies inside.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">95% confidence interval from 9.869 to\n14.131 mm; reference value 10 mm lies inside.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: 95% confidence interval from 9.869 to 14.131 mm;\nreference value 10 mm lies inside.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional summary of 16 independently sampled plants: mean 12 mm,\nsample standard deviation 4 mm. With 15 degrees of freedom, the 97.5th\npercentile is about 2.131; standard error is 1 mm. The 95% interval is\n[9.869, 14.131] mm. Against a reference of 10 mm,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.064<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx0.064<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.05<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0.05<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe null is not rejected; the difference of 2 mm remains an estimated\neffect. The interval includes 10 mm, corresponding to the same\ninferential decision in the matching two-sided test.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate standard error for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>64<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=64<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith unchanged\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmm.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.064<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\approx0.064<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprove equality with 10 mm?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why additionally report effect and confidence interval?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>64<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">4/\\sqrt{64}=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmm.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Failure to reject only means that the decision rule did not\nyield sufficient evidence against the null.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">They show magnitude and uncertainty; a p value alone does not\nindicate biological relevance.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>For small samples the normality assumption is important. Dependent\nobservations, severe outliers, selective reporting and multiple testing\nmay distort inference. Sample size alone does not guarantee reliability.\nEstablishing equivalence requires a separate prespecified procedure and\na scientifically justified tolerance margin.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Inference requires a model, an effect and a transparent decision.\nM2-L5 considers how study design constrains those conclusions.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/8-2-a-single-population-mean-using-the-student-t-distribution\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Statistics 2e, 8.2 A Single Population Mean using the\nStudent t Distribution<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Student t confidence interval · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/9-introduction\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Statistics 2e, Ch. 9 Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Hypothesis\ntesting, error types · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M2-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M2-L5 – Experimental design and sampling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics, Statistics and Data Literacy<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M1&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M1-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Define target population and\nsample; distinguish random selection from random assignment and analyze\nblocked designs.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>An ecological study aims to estimate whether shading affects plant\ngrowth. Soil type and moisture vary spatially. If all shaded plants are\nstudied in the wet area and all controls in the dry area, the effect of\nshade cannot be separated from the effect of soil.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<p>The target population is the set of units to which a claim is\nintended to apply. The sample contains the units actually observed; a\nsampling frame defines which units can be reached. In simple random\nsampling, equal-sized subsets have the same selection probability.\nStratified samples draw units from prespecified subpopulations; cluster\nsamples select groups of units, requiring dependence within those groups\nto be considered.<\/p>\n<p>Random <strong>selection<\/strong> supports generalization to a\npopulation; random <strong>assignment<\/strong> of conditions supports\ncausal comparisons. They have distinct functions. In a randomized block\ndesign, comparable units are grouped, for instance by site, and\nconditions randomly assigned within each block. Paired analysis uses\nwithin-block differences. Repeated measurements on the same unit do not\nautomatically increase the number of independent units. Sample size\nplanning requires a scientifically relevant effect size, an estimate of\ndispersion, a decision rule and desired power in advance.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_010_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Four sites, each with two conditions and their paired differences.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Four sites, each with two conditions and\ntheir paired differences.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Four sites, each with two conditions and their paired\ndifferences.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Four fictional independent sites each contain two randomly assigned\nplots. Growth in cm under shade is 4, 5, 3, 6 and without shade 6, 5, 6,\n7. Paired differences (shaded minus unshaded) are −2, 0, −3, −1 cm;\ntheir mean is −1.5 cm. Four differences are available for blockwise\nanalysis. Treating the eight individual measurements as eight\nindependently drawn differences would be wrong. This illustrates\nanalysis structure, not an established environmental effect.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What quantity is analyzed in a paired comparison?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is one possible advantage of stratification by soil type when\nselecting sites?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Do 20 measurements per plot turn the study into 160 independent\nplots?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For each site, the difference between the two assigned\nconditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Relevant soil types can be deliberately represented and described\nseparately; weighting and selection must suit the target\npopulation.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Measurements are nested within plots; independent units and\nblocking remain decisive.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>Not all ecological factors can be manipulated. Observational studies\nmay support hypotheses but require additional assumptions for causal\nclaims. Missing observations and nonrandom dropout can impair even a\ncarefully planned design.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>Good sampling and experimental designs specify the statistical unit\nand scope. The next lesson connects these decisions to reproducible\nanalyses.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L6<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/reproducibility/principles-guidelines-reporting-preclinical-research\">NIH\n– Principles and Guidelines for Reporting Preclinical Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nTransparency; Replicates; Randomization; Blinding; Inclusion and\nexclusion · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/1-1-the-science-of-biology\">OpenStax\n– Biology 2e, 1.1 The Science of Biology<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · The Process of Science;\nScientific Reasoning · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/1-2-data-sampling-and-variation-in-data-and-sampling\">OpenStax\n– Introductory Statistics 2e, 1.2 Data, Sampling, and Variation in Data\nand Sampling<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Random, stratified and cluster sampling; variation in\nsamples · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_biology_en:M2-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M2-L6 – Reproducible data analysis<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M2 – Mathematics, Statistics and Data Literacy<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Bachelor<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L3<\/a> to <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M2&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M2-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Design a transparent data\nworkflow with preserved raw data, a data dictionary, documented cleaning\nand executable analysis.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Introduction<\/h2>\n<p>A mean in a report is hard to verify if it is unclear which\nobservations were used, which units converted and which rows excluded.\nReproducible data analysis connects a reported value to its\nderivation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Principles and model<\/h2>\n<p>Raw data are preserved unchanged. A data dictionary describes\nvariable names, units, encodings, allowed values and missing entries. A\nmachine-readable table uses a clear observational unit per row, unique\nkeys and separate variables per column. Quality checks examine, for\nexample, value ranges, duplicate keys, units and missing data. Cleaning\nsteps are recorded as distinct reproducible transformations; an unusual\nvalue is not removed without scientific justification.<\/p>\n<p>An analysis script reads the data, checks expected conditions,\ngenerates derived values and saves tables and figures. The runtime\nenvironment, library versions, input files and parameters are\ndocumented. In stochastic procedures, a fixed random seed can help\nreproduce a run; software versions may still produce different results.\nA hash can detect file changes but proves neither truth nor correct\nprovenance. For personal or otherwise protected data, reproducibility\nmust comply with relevant access rules.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-biology-en/media/img/U_BIO_011_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Preserved raw data pass through documented processing and scripts to a verifiable report.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Preserved raw data pass through\ndocumented processing and scripts to a verifiable report.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Preserved raw data pass through documented processing and\nscripts to a verifiable report.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Application<\/h2>\n<p>Fictional measurements in cm: A=4, B=6, C=missing, D=8. Missing is\nnot zero. An executable Python example with no additional libraries\npreserves this distinction:<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>raw <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;A&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;B&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;C&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;D&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>)]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>({k <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> k, _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> raw}) <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(raw)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>values <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> [v <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _, v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> raw <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">is<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">not<\/span> <span class=\"va\">None<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">all<\/span>(v <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> v <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> values)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(values), <span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(raw)<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(values), <span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(values)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(values))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<p>Output: <code>3 1 6.0<\/code>. Three observed values and one missing\nvalue were counted; the mean of observed values is 6 cm. This does not\nexplain why C is missing. Report both counts and the exclusion rule.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-assessment<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What would the mean be if the missing value were wrongly encoded as\nzero?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What information must a data dictionary provide for the measurement\ncolumn?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is a hash alone insufficient for scientific quality\nassessment?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<details class=\"lc-solutions\" data-lc-section=\"S4\"><summary>Show solutions<\/summary>\n<div>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(4+6+0+8)/4=4.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncm; that would be a different and unjustified analysis here.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">At least meaning, unit, value range, encoding and handling of\nmissing entries.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It shows file equality or changes, not validity, measurement quality\nor ethical acceptability.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<\/div>\n<\/details>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Assumptions and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>The example uses complete-case analysis for a single variable. If\nmissingness is systematically related to the measured quantity, this\napproach may be biased. More complex methods require justified\nassumptions and do not replace investigation of why observations are\nmissing.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and transition<\/h2>\n<p>M2 connects mathematical models, statistical inference, experimental\ndesign and transparent data processing. These methods are used again in\nlater biology modules.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Continue:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_biology&amp;module=M3&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M3-L1<\/a><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.wissenschaftliche-integritaet.de/de/ll-12-389682\">DFG\n– Leitlinie 12: Dokumentation<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Dokumentation / Documentation · Last\naccessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/reproducibility/principles-guidelines-reporting-preclinical-research\">NIH\n– Principles and Guidelines for Reporting Preclinical Research<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> ·\nTransparency; Replicates; Randomization; Blinding; Inclusion and\nexclusion · Last accessed: 2026-11-25<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
